-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Hardware Compatibility**: The implementation assumes DX11 hardware. Mobile support is uncertain and likely unsupported [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Density Field Bottleneck**: The example generates the density field on the CPU. For real-time applications, this should be replaced with a `RenderTexture` generated on the GPU [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Buffer Overflow**: The append buffer capacity is fixed. If the number of triangles exceeds the allocated capacity, data will be lost or cause errors. The capacity is estimated as $(Resolution - 1)^3 \times 5$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Lighting**: The default shader uses a barebone diffuse lighting model. Custom shaders are required for advanced rendering [S1, unofficial].
+**Parameters:**
+*   `Resolution` ($N$): The grid resolution. Used for buffer sizing and dispatch. Typical values depend on memory capacity [S1, unofficial].
+*   `isoLevel`: The scalar threshold. Default is 0.5 [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Boundary Handling:** Threads with coordinates $\ge N-1$ must return immediately to prevent out-of-bounds access [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Hardware Support:** The implementation assumes DX11 hardware; mobile support is uncertain [S1, unofficial].
+*   **CPU Bottleneck:** If the density field is generated on the CPU, it may become a bottleneck. Using `RenderTexture` instead of `Texture3D` is recommended for real-time applications [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel is processed in parallel by a thread group [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the input texture and output buffer. The output buffer size is fixed based on the maximum possible triangles [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance**: Performance depends on GPU compute capabilities and the resolution of the volume. The use of append buffers allows for variable output size but may introduce synchronization overhead [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Real-time extraction, parallel processing, integration with Unity's rendering pipeline [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: CPU-bound density generation in the example, fixed buffer capacity, DX11 dependency [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use**: Real-time volumetric visualization in Unity applications on DX11-capable hardware [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use**: Mobile platforms (likely), applications requiring dynamic density generation on the GPU without additional setup, or when higher precision is needed than the fixed buffer capacity allows [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ for processing all voxels, where $N$ is the resolution. The parallel nature allows near-linear scaling with the number of GPU cores [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ for the input texture and $O(N^3)$ for the output buffer (worst-case 5 triangles per voxel) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Performance depends heavily on the GPU's compute capability and the efficiency of the append buffer implementation. Indirect rendering minimizes CPU-GPU synchronization overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** High parallelism, flexible output management via append buffers, and integration with modern graphics APIs [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires DX11 or higher; potential memory pressure from large output buffers; CPU density generation can be a bottleneck if not offloaded [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time isosurface extraction in Unity or similar engines where CPU processing is insufficient [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Mobile devices with uncertain DX11 support or scenes requiring extremely high resolution where memory bandwidth becomes a bottleneck [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This implementation was developed by Pavel Kouril for Unity 5.6, with potential compatibility with older versions [S1, unofficial]. It is based on the Marching Cubes algorithm and lookup tables by Paul Bourke [S1, unofficial]. The gradient normals calculation is based on work from "MCImproved" [S1, unofficial]. The project is hosted on GitHub as `pavelkouril/unity-marching-cubes-gpu` [S1, unofficial].
+This implementation was developed for Unity 5.6, leveraging the Marching Cubes algorithm by Paul Bourke and gradient-based normal calculation techniques [S1, unofficial]. It adapts the classic algorithm for GPU compute shaders, using append buffers for dynamic triangle emission and indirect rendering for efficient draw calls [S1, unofficial].
 
